<h1>The Celts (1992)</h1>

                <a class="new-amazon-album-link" href="https://amzn.to/3UBLYaD" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">
                    <Image  src={tc} alt="The Celts (1992)"  width="300" height="300" /></a>
                
                <p><strong><em>The Celts</em></strong>, reissued on <strong>16 November 1992</strong>, is a remastered edition of <strong>Enya</strong>'s 1987 self-titled debut studio album <em><strong><a href="/enya-album/">Enya</a></strong></em>.</p>

                <p>It contains music written and recorded for the soundtrack of the 1986 BBC documentary television series <a href="/the-celts-documentary/"><em>The Celts</em></a>.</p>

                <p><a href="https://amzn.to/3WeCL5y" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">Stream or download Enya songs</a></p>

                <h2>Commissioning and Recording History</h2>

                <p>In 1985, BBC producer <strong>Tony McAuley</strong> invited Enya to compose a single track for an upcoming documentary series about Celtic history.</p>

                <p>Enya submitted <strong>"March of the Celts"</strong>. Series director <strong>David Richardson</strong> loved the track so much that he commissioned her to score the entire six-part series.</p>

                <p>Working at <strong>Aigle Studio</strong> in Dublin and <strong>BBC Studios</strong> in London, Enya recorded 72 minutes of music alongside producer <strong>Nicky Ryan</strong> and lyricist <strong>Roma Ryan</strong>.</p>

                <h2>1992 Reissue and Chart Success</h2>
                
                <p>In 1992, following Enya's massive global commercial success with <i><a href="/watermark/">Watermark</a></i> (1988) and <i><a href="/shepperd-moons/">Shepherd Moons</a></i> (1991), <strong>Warner Music</strong> acquired the rights to her debut.</p>

                <p>The album was remastered by <strong>Arun Chakraverty</strong> and featured brand-new artwork designed by <strong>Sooky Choi</strong> with photography by <strong>David Scheinmann</strong>.</p>

                <p>Reissued by <strong>WEA</strong> in Europe and <strong>Reprise Records</strong> in the United States, <i>The Celts</i> outperformed its original sales performance.</p>

                <p>It reached a new peak of <strong>No. 10</strong> on the <strong>UK Albums Chart</strong>, returning to the charts in 1993, 1996, and 1998.</p>

                <p>In the United States, the album sold over <strong>1,000,000 copies</strong>, achieving <strong>Platinum certification</strong> from the RIAA.</p>

                <h2>Tracklist Variations</h2>
                
                <p>The 1992 reissue replaced the original 1:23 instrumental <strong>"Portrait"</strong> with a longer version titled <strong>"Portrait: Out Of The Blue"</strong> (3:11).</p>

                <p>Key variations across release formats include:</p>

                <ul>
                    <li><strong>1987 Original (Enya)</strong>: Features the short 1:23 version of <strong>"Portrait"</strong>.</li>
                    <li><strong>1992 Reissue (The Celts)</strong>: Includes the extended 3:11 track <strong>"Portrait (Out of the Blue)"</strong>, originally released as a B-side to <strong>"Orinoco Flow"</strong>.</li>
                    <li><strong>2009 Japanese SHM-CD</strong>: Features <strong>"Eclipse"</strong> (1:33) as an exclusive bonus track.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Sampling Legacy of "Boadicea"</h2>
                
                <p>The instrumental song <strong>"Boadicea"</strong> became one of the most heavily sampled melodies in hip-hop, R&amp;B, and dance music history.</p>

                <p>Major adaptations of the track include:</p>

                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The Fugees (1996)</strong>: Sampled the song on <strong>"Ready or Not"</strong> for <i>The Score</i>. Enya prepared a copyright lawsuit, but settled out of court for credit and royalties after learning it was not gangsta rap.</li>
                    <li><strong>Mario Winans featuring Enya &amp; P. Diddy (2004)</strong>: Sampled the melody for <strong>"I Don't Wanna Know"</strong>. Producer <strong>P. Diddy</strong> sought permission directly, granting Enya 60% royalties and co-artist credit.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pirates featuring Shola Ama (2004)</strong>: Adapted the hook for the answer song <strong>"You Should Really Know"</strong>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Salvatore Ganacci (2016)</strong>: Sampled the track on the electronic dance single <strong>"Dive"</strong>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Metro Boomin, The Weeknd &amp; 21 Savage (2022)</strong>: Featured the melody prominently on their hit single <strong>"Creepin'"</strong>.</li>
                </ul>

