			<h2 id="linernotes">Liner notes</h2>
                
                <h3>The Celts</h3>
                
                <p>As this is primarily a soundtrack project, we felt it would be appropriate to include the stories, images and thoughts behind the various themes which embrace this album.</p>

                <p>They represent histories, mythologies, impressions, hopes and our individual interpretations of them.</p>
                
                <h3>Aldebaran</h3>
                
                <p>The Red giant Star, <strong>Aldebaran</strong>, found in the constellation of <strong>Taurus</strong>, is the Eye of the bull.</p>

                <p>From the Arabic <strong>Al Dabaran</strong>, it means 'the follower' as it "follows" the <strong>Pleiades</strong> star cluster.</p>

                <p>This piece portrays future Celts passing Aldebaran on their journey to new territories, continuing their ancient migratory pattern.</p>
                
                <h3>I Want Tomorrow</h3>
                
                <p><em>...thoughts of the present...</em></p>

                <p>Commissioned to portray the concept of being spellbound by Queen Boudica.</p>
                
                <h3>March of the Celts</h3>
                
                <p><em>...echoes from the past...</em></p>

                <p>Recorded prior to the BBC series, this piece served as the demonstration track that won Enya the full scoring project.</p>
                
                <h3>Deireadh an Tuath</h3>
                
                <p>Translating from Irish as <strong>"End of the Tribe"</strong>, this piece invokes incantations that the earth may once again be fertile.</p>

                <p><strong>Samhain</strong>, the greatest Celtic festival marking their New Year on <strong>31st October</strong>, is now celebrated as Halloween.</p>
                
                <h3>The Sun in the Stream</h3>
                
                <p>Inspired by the legend of the <strong>Salmon of Knowledge</strong>.</p>

                <p>In the Grove of the nine wise Hazel trees, the <strong>River Boyne</strong> flowed from the sacred pool.</p>

                <p>The salmon feasted on crimson nuts fallen from hazel trees, gaining all the truth and wisdom in the world.</p>
                
                <h3>To Go Beyond</h3>
                
                <p><em>...reflection on the future...</em></p>
                
                <h3>Fairytale</h3>
                
                <p>Based on the Irish legend of Fairy King <strong>Midir</strong> and his love for the beautiful princess <strong>Étaín</strong>.</p>

                <p>A tale of love, jealousy, and endurance, Étaín is banished and transformed into a butterfly, reborn as a mortal <strong>1,012 years</strong> later.</p>
                
                <h3>Epona</h3>
                
                <p><strong>Epona</strong> was revered as the Celtic <strong>Horse Goddess</strong> and is closely identified with <strong>Rhiannon</strong>.</p>

                <p>Depicted with birds and a cornucopia, her magical birds were reputed to lull the living to sleep and awaken the dead.</p>
                
                <h3>Triad (Saint Patrick)</h3>
                
                <p>Captured by Celtic raiders as a boy, <strong>Saint Patrick</strong> spent six years in captivity before escaping and returning as a priest to convert Ireland.</p>

                <p>The track adapts the ancient Latin and Irish hymn <strong>"Deus Meus Adiuva Me"</strong>.</p>

                <h3>Cú Chulainn</h3>
                
                <p><strong>Cú Chulainn</strong> means "hound of Culann". As a child, he was named <strong>Setanta</strong> ("the little one").</p>

                <p>He was renamed after killing the watchdog of the smith Culann, offering to guard the smith's house in place of the dog.</p>

                <p>His legendary exploits are recounted in the epic saga <strong>Táin Bó Cúailnge</strong> (The Cattle Raid of Cooley).</p>
                
                <h3>Oisin</h3>
                
                <p><strong>Oisín</strong> ("little fawn") journeyed to <strong>Tír na nÓg</strong> (the Land of Youth) with <strong>Niamh Cinn Óir</strong> ("Niamh of the Head of Gold").</p>

                <p>He stayed for <strong>300 years</strong> without aging, but upon returning to Ireland and stepping on the earth, he aged instantly and died.</p>
                
                <h3>Portrait</h3>
                
                <p><em>Out of the Blue...</em></p>
                
                <h3>Boadicea</h3>
                
                <p><strong>Boadicea</strong> (or <strong>Boudicca</strong>), meaning "Victorious", was Queen of the <strong>Iceni tribe</strong> of East Anglia.</p>

                <p>She led a major rebellion in <strong>60 A.D.</strong> against Roman forces, destroying Colchester and St. Albans and capturing London.</p>

                <p>Defeated in battle, she poisoned herself to avoid humiliation by the Romans.</p>
                
                <h3>Bard Dance</h3>
                
                <p>In ancient Celtic times, the <strong>Bard</strong> held tremendous importance in praising the king through eulogy or satire.</p>

                <p>Alongside the <strong>Druid</strong>, <strong>Warrior</strong>, and <strong>Silversmith</strong>, the Bard represents the quintessential image of ancient Celts.</p>
                
                <h3>Dan y Dŵr</h3>
                
                <p>Translating from Welsh as <strong>"Under the Water"</strong>, this is a lament for the submerged village of <strong>Capel Celyn</strong> in Wales.</p>

                <p>The village was flooded despite fierce protests to build the <strong>Llyn Celyn</strong> reservoir, burying its history beneath the water.</p>
                
